# {{PROJECT_NAME}} — Design

This is the **doc-of-record** for how {{PROJECT_NAME}} looks and behaves. When the implementation
and this document disagree, this document wins until someone deliberately changes it. Two tools read
it as source of truth — the `design-auditor` agent and `/audit-tokens` — so it is not a summary of
the design system, it *is* the design system's written form.

Maintained by whoever changes the presentation layer. See §"Definition of Done" for what that
obliges.

---

## How much of this document this project owns

This is the design doc of a **product that renders an interface** — an `app`, in the archetype
vocabulary. It is not scaffolded for a design-system package or a documentation site
(`templates/docs/roles.json` marks the `design` role `na` for both): in a design-system repository
the visual system, the component pages and the token reference are separate documents — the
`tokens`, `theming`, `components` and `patterns` roles — and folding them back into one file is the
conflation this template used to carry (issue #95). If you are reading this in a package, map those
roles instead.

Even in an app, this template covers the whole surface a design doc *can* cover, and most apps own
only part of it. **Answer this first, because it determines which sections below you fill in and
which you replace with a link.**

| Where the shared visual system lives | This document owns | It links out for |
|---|---|---|
| In this app | Principles, tokens, component specs, reuse rules, accepted exceptions, the screen inventory | Nothing — but if a dedicated role exists here, link to it rather than restating it |
| In a shared design system this app consumes | Principles, reuse rules, accepted exceptions, the screen inventory | Tokens, component specs, theming — owned by the design system |

